{"id": "21984", "revid": "1423298", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=21984", "title": "Mendi", "text": "Mendi is the capital of the Southern Highlands province in the Highlands region of Papua New Guinea. Mendi is also little town in Ethiopia, Oromia regional state in Western Wollegga district.\nUnderstand.\nMendi was known as \"Murumbu\" during the colonial era in the 1950s but gradually got its current name after independence. Mendi is at an altitude of 1,675 m and lies in the Mendi River valley on a gentle volcanic slope. The population in Mendi for 2021 is 56 108. Mendi is the third largest of Papua New Guinea's 41 cities. Mendi's residents live in clans with long-standing relationships with people living elsewhere (Kandep, Ialibu, Tambul, Lai valley and Kagua). \nThe valley is flanked to the north-east by Mount Giluwe, PNG\u2019s second highest mountain and to the west by limestone peaks that separate it from the Lai Valley. In addition to the town and the river, \u201cMendi\u201d also refers to the people who live in the surrounding areas. There are two rivers that flow through Mendi named as River Mendi and Magani River which collectively combine and flows southerly to meet River Lai and on towards Purari River in the Gulf Province. Temperatures in the area range from 7\u00b0 to 24\u00b0 C, with high-altitude areas regularly experiencing crop-damaging frosts. Vegetables and coffee are the main crops. \nIn 2000 the town suffered major inter-tribal violence, when around 50 people were killed. Although it has been relatively quiet since then, tour organizers tend to shun it and the neighbouring township of Tari. This is very much a location for the independent, adventurous traveller.\nGet in.\nMendi is on the southern spur of the Highlands Highway. It can be reached by road from Lae, Goroka, Mt. Hagen, Madang and Tari."}
{"id": "21985", "revid": "2441334", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=21985", "title": "Mendips", "text": "The Mendips are a range of hills in northern Somerset in the south-west of the United Kingdom. The area is designated as an Area of Outstanding Natural Beauty (AONB).\nUnderstand.\nThe Mendips are carboniferous limestone hills, situated a few miles south of Bristol, just north of the Somerset Levels. There have been quarries and mining here from early times, all mining has now ceased. Caving, climbing and hiking are popular outdoor activities.\nThe caves at Cheddar contain traces of Palaeolithic man. Lead, copper, silver were mined from the Bronze Age, through Roman times up until the 19th century.\nThe tops of the Mendips consist mostly of grassland, grazed by sheep and there is a wide variety of plant and animal life for those interested in natural history.\nTowns and villages in the Mendips include:\nGet in.\nBy plane.\nBristol Airport () is well served from most of Europe and is a few miles north.\nBy train.\nThe nearest railway stations are at Bath, Bristol, Weston super Mare and Frome.\nBy car.\nFairly easily accessible from the M5 motorway, Bristol, Bath, Wells, and Weston-super-Mare. The A38 between Bristol and the M5 passes the western side of the hills and passes Bristol Airport.\nGet around.\nBy bus.\nThe Chew Valley Explorer bus serves many of the local villages. Some other bus services are provided by First Bus as above. Otherwise you have to walk or use a car.\nOn foot.\nMany of the more interesting destinations are only accessible on foot. Walking guides are available from the Mendip Hills Area of Outstanding Natural Beauty service.\nEat.\nMany of the pubs serve good food, there are cafes and restaurants in Cheddar and Wells.\nDrink.\nMost villages have pubs amongst which are:\nSleep.\nThere are a number of guest houses, also some self-catering accommodation, camping and caravan sites and a few independent hotels.\nStay safe.\nOutdoor activities such as climbing, caving and abseiling should only be undertaken with suitably qualified groups. The hills contain many old mine workings and even undiscovered pot-holes, so walkers should be well equipped and use good maps.\nGo next.\nOther local attractions near the Mendips are:"}
{"id": "21986", "revid": "2441334", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=21986", "title": "Mendocino", "text": "A favorite weekend getaway for Northern California Bay Area residents, the village of Mendocino is older than both the county and state to which it belongs, having lent its name to the former and further still to the combined coastal watersheds that occupy the nearly 200 miles (320 km) between the mouths of the Eel and Russian Rivers, known far and wide as the \"Mendocino Coast\". The town has long been a haven for artists, and is famous for its art galleries as well as its restaurants and trendy shops. It is smaller and more \"refined\" than its blue collar neighbor to the North, Fort Bragg.\nUnderstand.\nThe city is sometimes called Mendocino Village to more clearly differentiate it from Mendocino County.\nGet in.\nBy plane.\nThe closest international airport is San Francisco (SFO), though when accounting for traffic the smaller hubs in Oakland (OAK) and Sacramento (SMF) can often be reached more quickly and offer slightly cheaper fares. The greatest convenience is found in Santa Rosa at the Charles M. Schulz \u2013 Sonoma County Airport (STS), though it, too, is still a nearly two hour drive away. Little River Airport (KLLR) is a small, county-owned public-use airport located just four miles southeast of the village and charter flights are available from Surf Air, among others, however the costs are prohibitive, averaging over $4,000 round trip for a three-seat craft to any of the smaller airports on the periphery of the Bay Area or Sacramento.\nBy car.\nFrom the Bay Area, the most scenic drive to Mendocino is on coastal Highway 1, winding through western Marin County and Sonoma County. A faster but less impressive alternative is to take highway 101 to the junction with tiny state Highway 128 just north of Cloverdale, then 128 to 1, about 2 miles south of the town proper. This route takes you through the Anderson Valley, which is one of Mendocino County's main wine-growing regions. Mendocino is about 165 miles north of San Francisco.\nFrom the north, it's easiest to take Highway 1 south from highway 101 at near Garberville, through Fort Bragg. An alternate is taking state highway 20 west from Willits to Fort Bragg, then south to Mendocino.\nGet around.\nThe town is easily walkable, but you will want a car for visiting nearby beaches and sights. The Headlands, the bluffs on the west end of town, are only a half-mile from the eastern part of town, which is only several blocks from north to south. Mountain bikes are another fun option.\nThe historic downtown area, where most of the interesting shops and restaurants are, can be a bit challenging to negotiate for people with mobility limitations. Some places have steep curbs or a couple of steps, and the sidewalks can be steeper than expected. However, alternate routes will almost always get you to your destination. \nSee.\nA favorite pastime from mid-December to early May is whale watching. Pods of grey whales are visible off the coast, migrating to and from their winter calving grounds off the coast of Baja California.\nBuy.\nMendocino's walkable core is full of charming shops.\nSleep.\nThere are several inns in the Mendocino Village. In addition to the many Bed and Breakfasts in town, there are a number of rental properties in the area. These tend to be booked on holiday and summer weekends, but many bargains are available off season."}
{"id": "21988", "revid": "132185", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=21988", "title": "Mendocino County", "text": "Mendocino County borders the Pacific Ocean on California's North Coast. The county is located halfway between San Francisco and the Oregon border, and boasts redwood forests, wineries, breweries, and remote, untouched coastline.\nGet in and around.\nMendocino Transit Authority (MTA) provides limited bus service into and around Mendocino County. But unless you're willing to carefully plan your trip around the bus schedules, driving is probably the best option for getting around this sparsely populated region.\nThe nearest passenger airports are Sonoma County Airport ('; limited flights only) and, a bit further south, San Francisco International Airport (') and Oakland International Airport ().\nSee.\nIn addition to redwood trees, the following attractions exist within Mendocino County:\nDrink.\nMendocino County has many wineries and several breweries as well.\nStay safe.\nDo not venture off hiking trails or roads. Clandestine marijuana grows are in the drier warmer areas of the county due to the relaxed marijuana laws."}
{"id": "21989", "revid": "2441334", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=21989", "title": "Mendoza", "text": "\"For other places with the same name, see (disambiguation).\"\nMendoza is a city in western Argentina, in the desert Cuyo region. Mendoza is the center of the Argentinian wine industry, for which it is world renowned. It is also near the Aconcagua, the highest mountain outside of the Himalayas. Mendoza is the capital of the province of Mendoza.\nUnderstand.\nAlthough it is in an extremely dry desert region, Mendoza has an extensive artificial irrigation system, which allows for greenery throughout the city as well as the growth of grapes used to make its wines. Most streets have irrigation channels on either side, with bridges for pedestrian and vehicular traffic. These are periodically flooded with water diverted from the river. The trees and the wide avenues give the city a beautiful ambience, a change from much of the bare feel of many Argentine cities.\nTo the immediate west is the Pre-Cordillera of the Andes towering over the city, with peeks at some of the snow-covered (throughout the year) Andes peaks beyond.\nA siesta, or afternoon nap, is still taken in Mendoza. Most businesses close approximately 13:00-17:00, then re-open until about 20:30-21:30. Banks are only open in the mornings M-F 09:00-13:00.\nClimate.\nSummers can be hot and dry in the city. January is particularly hot; temperatures of 40\u00b0C (104\u00b0F) are not uncommon. However, the lack of humidity makes both the heat and cool more bearable than, say, humid Buenos Aires. The nearby mountains are cool, though, even in the summer.\nWinters are moderately cold in the city from late June to late August, and very cold in the mountains. Many ski centres are located near Mendoza (see #Do below).\nThe Zonda wind, a wind characterized by warm, dry air descending from the mountains frequently occurs in the winter, causing temperatures to raise as much as 20\u00b0C (36\u00b0F) in a few hours. These events can cause temperatures to be warm to hot, even in the middle of winter.\nGet in.\nBy bus.\nThe large bus terminal is about 2 km east of the city centre. Taxis and \"remis\" (private taxis) are readily available (USD3-4 to the centre), or it's a 15-minute walk (not recommended at night, the area between it and the centre borders on the red light district).\nThere are daily bus connections to all major destinations including Bariloche and Santiago, a beautiful 7-hour bus ride crossing the Andes. Santiago is not always reachable by bus as the Andes pass closes after the first heavy snowfall in the winter months, normally around late May, but when it does snow heavily, the pass is usually only closed for a few days at most. The joint immigration/customs control for Chile-out-stamps/Argentina-in-stamps (convenient) for entry into Argentina is at Los Horcones near Puente del Inca, and the one for entry-into-Chile/exit-from-Argentina stamps is at Las Libertadores in Chile, 5\u00a0km past the tunnel. The waiting time at the border which may be very long (up to 8 hours during high season).\nBus travel times to/from Mendoza:\nBy plane.\nFrom the airport, you can take a remis (a type of taxi) for fixed posted prices (AR$225 to the centre in 2019). There is also a city bus (collectivo) that takes you downtown, but it comes only every 40 minutes and takes an hour to make its way downtown (but you need to buy a bus card, not available at airport).\nMendoza is a travel hub of sorts for Argentina.\nBuses from Buenos Aires: Micros de Retiro.\nGet around.\nCentral Mendoza is relatively compact and walkable: for example, it's a 20- to 30-minute walk from Plaza Independencia to Parque San Martin. However to get to the bodegas (vineyards) to the south, walking isn't recommended as it they are at least 10 km away.\nIn January 2019, there was the radical transformation of the public transport system, introducing Mendotran, a system which is based on the setting up of two different transport \"webs\": one that takes you into and out of the city proper, and one that connects the different areas of the periphery. Also be aware that bus stop signs may still be missing, and you'll have to rely on Mendotran's website, Google Maps, or the knowledge of people in the area (particularly store-keepers) to find your stop. As of May 2026 it's being expanded to travel North, up to the airport and also South, to Luj\u00e1n de Cuyo.\nBuses are plentiful, but a little confusing at first. A single ticket is AR$1200 as of May 2026, though it rises along with inflation every few months. You cannot pay cash for your fare, and you can either pay with a SUBE card (the same as in Buenos Aires and some other cities), or with your international contactless debit/credit card when boarding a bus. You can buy the SUBE card from some kioskos near a bus stop for AR$490 (March 2023), and charge it up at the same place. NFC Payments are also accepted as of 2026. An interactive map of the city bus routes can be found on this city website: city bus map. You may also use Google Maps or other map apps to find the best way to get somewhere. The new system relies heavily on the need to change buses to get to your destination, and so it is common for Google Maps to suggest doing just that. Depending on how long your first journey is, you may not have to pay for your second one: after touching in, you have a 90-min period during which it is free to get on another bus.\nThe Metrotranv\u00eda (MTM) is a modern electric tram-train system. The Green Line connects the city center with the south-eastern suburb of Guti\u00e9rrez in Maip\u00fa district and is being extended to Las Heras in the north of the metro area. It uses the same prepaid-card system than the buses, and combinations with buses can be made at no cost. \nTaxis are plentiful, use meters, and fairly cheap, costing about the same as in Buenos Aires. A trip from the bus terminal to Plaza Independencia will cost around AR$50 (May 2018)\nMendoza is one of the few cities in Argentina where Uber has a presence. Most times it's more convenient to use Uber than regular taxis.\nMendoza benefits from an impressive cycle lane network, probably the best in the country after BA and Cordoba.\nYou can hire bicycles in town. Most hostels can put you in touch with a bicycle hire outfit. Prices are negotiable (i.e. they will charge you as much as they think you are willing to pay) but you shouldn't pay more than AR$15000-20000 per day. You will need some form of ID to leave as deposit. Ask to see the bike \"before\" handing over your money; many are old clunkers.\nSee.\nAlternatively you can rent a bicycle at \"Bicis del Parque - Bike the Park!\". The area near the lake is the most manicured. \nDo.\nMany companies organize trekking, expeditions, horseback riding, and whitewater rafting in the desert and the mountains. Mountain cabins in areas with spectacular scenery are easily rentable in the city. Check the classified ads in the newspaper.\nLearn.\nAs with many cities in Argentina, there is a variety of Spanish courses and private lessons are available. There are two extablished language schools in Mendoza: Intercultural is the biggest, has a range of afternoon activities, and is slightly more expensive, Greenfields (aka COINED) is smaller and feels even less well organised, but many of the teachers work at both schools.\nAnother great option for individual or very small tailor-made quality group lessons with a highly trained instructor: Spanish in Mendoza, Argentina (SIMA).\nAnother interesting way to learn Spanish is by sharing accommodation. For people planning to stay for a couple of months, renting a room in a shared place could be the best option. Prices are reasonable low compared to hostels and hotels ranging from AR$700 in a student apartment to AR$130 for a homestay with no meals.\nEat.\nGood restaurants abound. For a round-up of Mendoza's more expensive eateries ask for the \"Gu\u00eda Mendoza Gourmet\" from the tourist office. The main restaurant strip is on Aristides Villanueva, which runs east-west from Ave Belgrano (where the defunct railway tracks are) to Parque San Martin. It is difficult to have a bad meal here, although as a general rule be wary of special offers from places near the hostels - they may be cheap, but this shows in the quality. There are also some excellent (and pricey) restaurants on Ave Sarmiento running west from Plaza Independencia. A cluster of cheaper restaurants are on Ave Juan B Justo\nTry world-famous Argentinian beef asado (roasted) from a parrilla (grill) restaurant, with a bottle of Mendoza's excellent wine. If you don't want a whole bottle of wine better to ask for a 375ml bottle - often called 3/8 of a litre in Argentina - rather than risking the house wine which is like house wine all over the world, pretty variable. Mendoza's most famous varieties are the Malbecs from Maip\u00fa and Luj\u00e1n de Cuyo. Other good options are Cabernet Sauvignons and Merlots.\nEven by Argentinian standards, Mendocinans eat late. On weekdays kitchens open around 21:00, but few diners arrive before 22:00. On Fridays and Saturdays things don't get going until 23:00.\nSleep.\nAlthough Mendoza is a very liveable city, and many choose to stay for a few weeks to take language courses and the like, there is not the same short term apartment rental infrastructure as in Buenos Aires. An internet search will bring up a few options but be wary of paying deposits before you arrive as the apartment may not live up to your expectations. Traffic noise can be a particular problem.\nThe most pleasant part of town is between Plaza Independencia and Park San Martin - with quiet streets and well kept neighbourhoods, and the bars and restaurants of Ar\u00edstides Villanueva within walking distance. East of the centre is the more low rent area, and contains the cheaper hostels.\nStay safe.\nBe wary of scams, especially around the bus terminal. Occasionally foreigners will pretend to have been robbed and use your sympathy to \"borrow\" money for a bus ride.\nBe careful arriving early morning on overnight buses. If you put your bags down, someone may try to take them.\nMaipu and Las Heras are something of a crime hot spot although locals say most of the problems are on the eastern side of the train line. Still it might pay not to wander around by yourself in Maipu at night after a long day wine tasting.\nAs everywhere in Argentina, be careful of the vehicles. They do not honour the right of way for pedestrians or stop-sign laws (the police just stand around and are mostly unhelpful). Intersections are death traps, this cannot be emphasized too much, the vehicles are usually driven erratically, fast, without attention, wandering and without signalling. Look everywhere, and make no assumptions. Especially be careful when there is a bus or taxi approaching from any direction. Many pedestrians choose to jaywalk (not a crime here) in the middle of the block to avoid endangering their lives and limbs at intersections!\nGo next.\nSan Juan, the quieter, greener, \"Ciudad Oasis\" is 150 km, about 2 hours, to the north. Bus departures every hour or two.\nDuring the December to March high season, July mid-winter break and holidays when bus usage is especially high, buy your bus tickets out at the terminal at least a few days before you leave."}
{"id": "21991", "revid": "160739", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=21991", "title": "Mendoza (disambiguation)", "text": "There is more than one place called Mendoza:"}
{"id": "21992", "revid": "2253620", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=21992", "title": "Mendoza (province)", "text": "Mendoza Province is one of Argentina's most important wine-producing regions and a major destination for mountain, nature, adventure, food and wine tourism.\nLocated in western Argentina, in the Cuyo region, Mendoza stretches from the irrigated agricultural oases in the east to the Andes along the Chilean border. The province is home to Aconcagua, the highest mountain in the Americas, major ski resorts, extensive vineyards, historic wineries, olive groves, rivers, reservoirs, hot springs and large protected natural areas.\nMendoza is particularly famous for Malbec, but its wine regions produce many other varieties and offer a wide range of winery visits, tastings, restaurants and accommodation.\nUnderstand.\nMendoza is one of Argentina's largest provinces by area. Its geography is dominated by the contrast between the dry plains and irrigated oases in the east and the Andes mountains in the west.\nThe province has a dry climate, with hot summers and generally low humidity. Agriculture is possible in the populated oases thanks to an extensive irrigation network fed by rivers descending from the Andes.\nWine is one of the defining elements of Mendoza's economy, landscape and culture. The province produces a large majority of Argentina's wine and is internationally associated with Malbec, although Cabernet Sauvignon, Bonarda, Syrah, Chardonnay, Sauvignon Blanc and other varieties are also important.\nMendoza's wine country is not a single compact area. The main wine regions include the traditional First Zone around Maip\u00fa and Luj\u00e1n de Cuyo, the Uco Valley, the Eastern Oasis and the Southern Oasis around San Rafael.\nDistances between wine regions can be considerable. A visitor planning winery visits should therefore choose a region according to the type of experience desired rather than assuming that all wineries are close together.\nWhen to visit.\nMendoza can be visited throughout the year.\nAutumn is particularly attractive for wine tourism because it coincides with the grape harvest and the landscape of the vineyards changes considerably.\nSpring and autumn generally offer comfortable temperatures for outdoor activities and winery visits.\nSummer can be very hot in the lower areas. It is a good season for combining wine tourism with mountain or water activities, but sun protection and hydration are important.\nWinter is the main season for snow and skiing in the mountains. High mountain roads can be affected by snow and ice.\nRegions.\nGreater Mendoza.\nGreater Mendoza includes the provincial capital and the metropolitan departments of Godoy Cruz, Guaymall\u00e9n, Las Heras, Maip\u00fa and Luj\u00e1n de Cuyo.\nIt is the main gateway to the province and contains a large concentration of hotels, restaurants, cultural attractions and tourist services.\nMaip\u00fa and Luj\u00e1n de Cuyo are the two traditional wine areas closest to the capital. Both combine vineyards and wineries with restaurants, olive-oil producers and other wine-tourism experiences.\nPrecordillera and High Andes.\nThe mountains west of Mendoza include Potrerillos, Uspallata, Puente del Inca and Aconcagua.\nThe area is suitable for trekking, climbing, rafting, horseback riding, skiing and other mountain activities.\nAconcagua reaches about 6,961 m and is the highest mountain in the Americas. The Provincial Aconcagua Park protects the mountain and its surroundings.\nWeather conditions in the Andes can change quickly. Check road conditions, park access and weather forecasts before setting out.\nUco Valley.\nThe Uco Valley consists mainly of Tunuy\u00e1n, Tupungato and San Carlos.\nIt is one of Argentina's best-known high-altitude wine regions, with vineyards against the Andes, modern wineries, boutique hotels and restaurants.\nNational Route 40 crosses the valley and connects several of its main destinations.\nEastern Oasis.\nThe Eastern Oasis includes San Mart\u00edn, Jun\u00edn, Rivadavia, Santa Rosa and La Paz.\nIt is an important agricultural and wine-producing area with a strong rural character and a long history of viticulture.\nSouthern Oasis.\nThe Southern Oasis includes San Rafael, General Alvear and Malarg\u00fce.\nIt combines wineries and vineyards with rivers, reservoirs, canyons, volcanic landscapes, protected areas and adventure tourism.\nGet in.\nBy plane.\nGovernor Francisco Gabrielli International Airport, commonly known as El Plumerillo (MDZ), is the main airport serving Mendoza.\nThere are domestic and international connections. Routes and frequencies change according to the season, so travellers should check current schedules before booking.\nBy bus.\nMendoza's main bus terminal has long-distance services to Buenos Aires, C\u00f3rdoba, San Juan, San Luis, San Rafael, Bariloche, Santiago de Chile and other destinations.\nBus travel times vary considerably depending on the destination and road or border conditions.\nBy car.\nMendoza is connected to the rest of Argentina by national and provincial highways.\nNational Route 7 connects Mendoza with Buenos Aires to the east and with Chile to the west.\nNational Route 40 crosses the province from north to south and is particularly useful for reaching the Uco Valley and southern Mendoza.\nRoad conditions should be checked before travelling into the Andes.\nFrom Chile.\nThe international Los Libertadores border crossing connects Mendoza with Chile through the Andes.\nSnow, ice, strong winds and other weather conditions can affect the crossing, particularly in winter.\nGet around.\nThe province is large and there is no single best form of transport for all destinations.\nPublic transport is useful within Greater Mendoza, particularly for travelling between the city and some metropolitan districts.\nFor winery visits, organised excursions, private drivers and rental cars are practical options, especially when visiting wineries in different areas.\nIn some wine-producing areas, wineries and vineyards can also be visited by bicycle.\nFor mountain destinations, check road conditions before leaving and carry appropriate clothing, water and other equipment.\nSee.\nMendoza city.\nMendoza is the provincial capital and the main cultural and service centre.\nPlaza Independencia is the largest central square and a useful starting point for exploring the city.\nParque General San Mart\u00edn is one of the city's principal green spaces. It contains gardens, tree-lined avenues, a lake and the historic park gates.\nThe Cerro de la Gloria is located within the western part of the park and is home to the Monument to the Army of the Andes.\nMendoza city is also a convenient base for day trips to the nearby wine regions and the Andes.\nHistory and heritage.\nMendoza has a strong connection with the history of Argentina's independence and the Army of the Andes.\nSites associated with General Jos\u00e9 de San Mart\u00edn and the crossing of the Andes can be found in Mendoza city, the Uspallata area and the Manzano Hist\u00f3rico.\nHigh mountain scenery.\nNational Route 7 crosses the Andes towards Chile and provides access to some of Mendoza's best-known mountain landscapes.\nPotrerillos, Uspallata, Puente del Inca and Aconcagua Provincial Park are among the best-known destinations along or near the mountain corridor.\nNature.\nMendoza contains a wide variety of landscapes, from irrigated agricultural oases to high mountains and volcanic deserts.\nThe Atuel Canyon, Valle Grande, Los Reyunos, El Nihuil, La Payunia and Laguna del Diamante are among the province's principal natural attractions.\nDo.\nWine tourism.\nWine tourism is one of Mendoza's principal tourist activities.\nThe province has more than 200 wineries open to tourism, offering different types of experiences including guided visits, tastings, wine-paired meals, picnics, sunset events and other activities.\nThe experience varies considerably between wine regions. A visitor should choose a region according to the wineries and activities they want to visit rather than trying to cover several distant areas in one day.\nWays to visit wineries.\nWineries can be visited in several ways:\nMany wineries require reservations, particularly for tastings, meals and guided visits. Check current arrangements directly with the winery before travelling.\nFor a one-day wine itinerary, concentrating on one wine-producing area is usually more practical than crossing large distances between wineries.\nMaip\u00fa is one of the most accessible wine areas from Mendoza city. Parts of the department have a relatively high concentration of wineries, olive-oil producers and restaurants, making it possible to build a full-day itinerary without travelling long distances between every stop.\nLuj\u00e1n de Cuyo also has wine-tourism routes suitable for cycling, while San Rafael offers wine experiences that can also be combined with bicycle touring.\nVisiting wineries by bicycle.\nCycling between wineries is possible in several wine-producing areas of Mendoza.\nDepending on the area and operator, visitors can rent bicycles, join an organised tour or follow a self-guided route.\nIn Maip\u00fa, specialist services such as Maip\u00fa Bikes offer bicycle-based wine-tourism experiences.\nCycling can be an enjoyable way to travel through the vineyards at a slower pace and combine several stops during the day. It is particularly practical in areas where wineries and other attractions are close to one another.\nBefore choosing a route, consider distance, road conditions, traffic, water availability, weather and the time required for winery visits.\nIf wine tasting is part of the itinerary, arrange a safe way back before starting. Do not drive after drinking alcohol.\nWineries and wine projects.\nMendoza has a large number of wineries open to visitors. Some are internationally recognised for their wines, while others are particularly important for their history, architecture or connection with the local wine tradition.\nAmong the best-known wine producers and wine-tourism projects are:\nMountain and adventure activities.\nMendoza offers many outdoor activities, including:\nMountain weather can change rapidly. For activities requiring technical equipment or specialised knowledge, use qualified and properly equipped operators.\nHot springs and wellness.\nTermas de Cacheuta is one of Mendoza's best-known thermal and wellness destinations. It is located in a mountain setting beside the Mendoza River and can be combined with visits to Potrerillos and the surrounding Andes foothills.\nSkiing and snow.\nMendoza has several winter destinations, with Las Le\u00f1as being the province's best-known ski resort.\nLocated in the Malarg\u00fce Department, Las Le\u00f1as is one of Argentina's major ski areas and is known for its high altitude, terrain and long ski season when snow conditions are favourable.\nPenitentes, in the high Andes near the Chilean border, was historically another important ski centre. Its current status and services can vary, so check the latest information before planning a trip.\nSnow conditions and mountain road access vary from year to year.\nRural tourism.\nThe irrigated agricultural oases around Mendoza offer rural experiences involving vineyards, olive groves, farms, local food and traditional agricultural landscapes.\nStargazing.\nMendoza's dry climate and clear skies can provide good conditions for stargazing, particularly outside the main urban areas.\nLearn.\nMendoza is one of Argentina's most important places for learning about wine production.\nWinery visits can explain the different stages of production, from vineyard management and harvesting to fermentation, ageing and bottling.\nThe province also has museums dedicated to wine, agriculture and local history.\nThe National Wine and Harvest Museum in Maip\u00fa occupies a historic building associated with the former Giol winery and preserves objects and material related to Mendoza's winemaking history.\nThe Wine Museum at Bodega La Rural contains a large collection of historical tools, machines and objects associated with wine production.\nSpanish-language courses and private lessons are also available in Mendoza city.\nEat.\nMendoza's cuisine is closely connected to its agricultural and wine-producing landscape.\nTypical foods and products include:\nMany wineries have restaurants offering multi-course meals, wine pairings, picnics and seasonal menus.\nFine dining and Michelin.\nMendoza has become an international gastronomic destination. The Michelin Guide's 2026 selection includes nine restaurants in Mendoza with one Michelin Star.\nThe Mendoza restaurants with one wtar are:\nCal also received a Michelin Green Star for its sustainability practices.\nThe Michelin selection also includes other recommended restaurants in the province.\nReservations are recommended for winery restaurants and high-end dining, particularly during weekends and holiday periods.\nDrink.\nWine is Mendoza's best-known drink, with Malbec being its most internationally recognised variety.\nOther important varieties include Cabernet Sauvignon, Bonarda, Syrah, Chardonnay and Sauvignon Blanc.\nMendoza also has an expanding craft-beer, vermouth and spirits scene.\nWine tastings are an important part of many tourist experiences. If several tastings are planned on the same day, arrange transportation before beginning the itinerary.\nBuy.\nTypical products to take home from Mendoza include:\nMany wineries sell wine directly from their premises, sometimes including labels that are difficult to find elsewhere.\nIf travelling by bicycle, consider how bottles and other purchases will be transported safely.\nSleep.\nMendoza has accommodation ranging from hostels and apartments to luxury hotels, wine lodges and rural properties.\nMendoza city and Greater Mendoza have the largest concentration of hotels and other accommodation.\nThe wine regions offer boutique hotels, vineyard lodges, rural accommodation and winery-based stays.\nMountain destinations have hotels, cabins, lodges and other accommodation oriented towards outdoor activities and winter sports.\nBecause the province is large, travellers staying for several days may find it more convenient to change accommodation rather than use Mendoza city as a base for every destination.\nStay safe.\nMendoza is a large province containing urban, rural and mountain environments.\nIn cities, use normal precautions with money, documents and electronic devices, particularly in crowded areas and around transport terminals.\nIn rural areas, pay attention to agricultural traffic and roads with limited pedestrian infrastructure.\nMountain weather can change rapidly. Check forecasts, road conditions and access restrictions before travelling into the Andes.\nCyclists should pay particular attention to road traffic and carry enough water, sun protection and appropriate safety equipment.\nIf an itinerary includes wine tastings, arrange safe transportation before drinking.\nFor emergencies, the general emergency number is 911.\nHealth.\nThe main cities in Mendoza have hospitals, clinics, pharmacies and emergency medical services.\nFor medical emergencies, 107 and 911 can be used.\nVisitors planning mountain activities should carry sufficient water, sun protection, suitable clothing and basic first-aid supplies.\nTravellers staying several days should consider travel insurance and carry any regular medication they require.\nContact.\nThe official provincial tourism website is Mendoza Tourism.\nIt provides information about destinations, wineries, gastronomy, accommodation, activities and events.\nBefore visiting an individual winery, restaurant, hotel or activity provider, check its current website or contact information because opening arrangements, reservations and services can change.\nFestivals.\nNational Grape Harvest Festival.\nThe National Grape Harvest Festival (Fiesta Nacional de la Vendimia) is Mendoza's best-known celebration of the wine harvest.\nIt takes place annually around the harvest season and includes official ceremonies, performances, parades and events in different parts of the province.\nOther events.\nMendoza also hosts gastronomic, cultural, musical and wine-related events throughout the year.\nDates and programmes vary from year to year, so check the current event calendar before travelling."}
{"id": "21994", "revid": "2441334", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=21994", "title": "Mengla", "text": "Mengla (Chinese: \u52d0\u814a, L\u00fc: \u19b5\u1999\u19b2\u19c2 \u199f\u19b1\u19c9) is a county in Xishuangbanna Dai Autonomous Prefecture, in southern Yunnan Province, China. Situated along the border with Laos and Myanmar, it is a gateway to the tropical landscapes and rich ethnic minority cultures of southwest China, featuring rainforest parks, rivers, botanical gardens, tea areas, and traditional Dai and other minority communities.\nUnderstand.\nGeography.\nMengla County lies in the southeastern part of Xishuangbanna Prefecture, bordering Laos to the east and Myanmar to the south and west. The county covers around 7,000 km\u00b2 with elevations ranging from lowland plains to forested hills and tropical rainforest.\nName and culture.\nThe name \"Mengla\" derives from the Dai language, with \"\u201cMeng\u201d\" meaning plains or region and \"\u201cLa\u201d\" referring to tea, reflecting the area\u2019s historic associations with tea cultivation. Early archaeological evidence shows human habitation here dating back to the Neolithic period.\nPeople.\nMengla is home to diverse ethnic groups including the Dai, Hani, Yi, Yao, Miao, Zhuang, Lahu and others, each with distinct traditions, festivals and cultural practices. \nClimate.\nMengla has a northern tropical humid monsoon climate with warm temperatures year-round. The region sees a distinct wet season (May to October) and dry season (November to April), making the dry months the most comfortable for travel.\nGet in.\nBy bus.\nBuses from Jinghong usually take 2\u00bd to 3 hours. Buses leave Jinghong between 07:00 and 18:00 and cost \u00a542.\nBy air.\nThe nearest airport is Xishuangbanna Gasa International Airport (JHG), near Jinghong City. From Jinghong, buses and taxis connect to Mengla County, taking around 2 hours. \nInternational border.\nThis is the first big town/city for travelers arriving from Laos. Buses from Luang Namtha in Laos take around 4 hours. From Luang Namtha you can get a bus leaving at around 08:00, going to Boten (Chinese border) and Mengla. You may need a Chinese visa beforehand as there is no way to get one on arrival if you do not qualify for a visa waiver or visa-free entry. The border is close (about one hour). Customs procedures may take up to an hour if busy. The trip costs about 45,000 kip.\nIt is best to get some local currency before-hand. There are a few touts changing money on both sides of the border and banks a few hundred metres away.\nGet around.\nThere are taxis and tuk-tuks that will take you around town for \u00a56. The train station is about 6 km south from the bus terminal and about 9 km from the town centre.\nGuanlei: There are ferry boats to Guanlei from Chiangsaen, Thailand four times a month.\nSee.\nMengla is the southern gateway to Xishuangbanna, surrounded by untouched rainforest good for trekking. Local Dai ethnic villages are within 10\u00a0km of the town that are untouched by commercial tourism. If you have the time and are not in a hurry to leave they are worth visiting. \nNew Bupan Aerial Skyway Park. The park gets its name from the skyway in the clouds attraction. Visitors literally walk through the canopy of the key rainforest species, Paraforestea, a tall, straight-trunked tree. You walk supported by cable-supported rope transoms that permit you to step along among the higher branches of the forest. It gives you a new perspective on the rainforest. Overall the park is easily accessed, clean and unique.\nYiwu (\u6613\u6b66), hometown of Pu'er Tea, is situated in the north of Mengla County. It is the hometown of Pu\u2019er Tea as well as the starting point of the Ancient Tea-Horse Trail (Road). In old times, Yiwu was known as a commercial city. It serves as the linkage between China and Laos and an important gateway to Southeast Asia.\nDo.\nMengla is a fast growing border town. There is a market in the mornings that runs parallel behind the main street. The place has become quite nice so you might be interested in simply walking around the place for a couple of hours.\nBuy.\nThere are several well stocked supermarkets in town. Two close to the bus station and more in the town centre. If you are in need of a Chinese SIM card there are several China Mobile outlets. Locally made items are also available at reasonable prices if your bargaining skills are good.\nEat.\nThere are many popular restaurants in the main shopping area in the town centre and no shortage of places to eat near the bus terminal. The restaurants all sell Yunnan and typical Chinese dishes but don't expect a very high standard. Rice noodle dishes (\u7c73\u7ebf) start from \u00a512 and larger or fancier meals priced upwards from there.\nDrink.\nThere is a newly developed bar area down by the river close to the centre of town, behind the DICO\u2019s chicken Western food outlet. There's also a bar area about two blocks form the bus terminal.\nThere doesn't seem to be anywhere to have a coffee in the bus terminal part of town. But in the centre there's several Chinese chain caf\u00e9s. The big chains Wi-Fi tends to need a Chinese phone number to log in, but smaller ones will just give you a password. Excellent coffee is at Bean House (\u8c46\u5c4b) in a cool street off the opposite side of the main road from the plaza, Qingnian Road.\nThe Wine Barrel Bar: As you walk towards Dico\u2019s turn right, large wine barrel above the entrance. This small bar serves cold beer, and is a relaxing place to sit and have a drink with its water feature and fish pond. A good sized bar, with live music from 21:00, dance floor, not too loud and a good place to mix with locals.\nSleep.\nThere are many hotels and guest house in Mengla, some options are: \nCheaper, as you leave the bus station and turn right, walk approx 50 meters and there is a guest house on the right hand side near the Forest Cafe. This place has no English name and is a pretty run-down establishment. They have rooms for around \u00a520-25 per night.\nConnect.\nA local guide, Peter Lee \u2013 +86 18608811499 \u2013 is a fluent English speaker - he can offer advice on places to stay, eat and drink. As you get off the bus in Mengla, turn right and walk down the street about 50 m to the Forest Cafe. He can also be found at his restaurant, which is a few restaurants left of the third intersection when you walk south from the north bus station.\nIn front of the bus station there is also a helpful man who will exchange money and also offer advice on accommodation and help you with directions. He takes a commission from places so be very specific about what you want and how much you want to pay. His exchange rates are poor but if you are in need of some Lao kip just to be safe, it is worth exchanging some euros, dollars, Thai baht or anything else if you need to. He has a very wide range of currencies and is keen to show them off. His English is also pretty good compared to most locals.\nStay safe.\nMengla County is generally considered safe for travelers. Mosquito repellent and sun protection are recommended during outdoor activities. Respect local customs, especially when visiting religious sites or minority settlements. Follow weather advisories during the rainy season. \nGo next.\nSouth into Laos.\nThe border crossing takes around an hour if you are on a bus. Much quicker if you are solo and the crossing is not busy.\nBuses depart to Louang Namtha in Laos at around 09:00 and take around 4 hours. Expect a bumpy and hair raising journey during the rainy season. The cost is around \u00a540.\nAlternatively, you can head to Muang Xay/Oudomxay, the largest city in northern Laos and then onto Luang Prabang.\nThere is now a direct bus from Jing Hong to Luang Namtha.\nNorth into China.\nThere are loads of buses to Jinghong, the capital of Xishuangbanna. The cost is around \u00a540. There are also 4 or 5 buses a day that leave for Kunming. The cost is about \u00a5250. Nowadays you can go to Yuangyang Terrace from Mengla by bus instead of going to Kunming directly, it is cheaper and less tiring."}
{"id": "21997", "revid": "2441877", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=21997", "title": "Menlo Park", "text": "Menlo Park is a suburban, tree-lined community in San Mateo County midway between the major cities of San Francisco and San Jose. Menlo Park offers visitors easy access to Stanford, Palo Alto and Silicon Valley. It is best known for its old-fashioned train station and downtown, and for being the home of venture capital and Meta (formerly Facebook). It also encompasses the USGS Headquarters, Veterans Administration Hospital, SRI Campus, and portions of the Stanford University campus, including the Stanford Linear Accelerator (SLAC).\nUnderstand.\nMenlo Park is relatively small (36,000 residents) 19 mi2, nestled between the San Francisco Bay (and US 101) in the north, and the Pacific Coast Mountain Range (and I-280) in the south. The town is divided in half by El Camino Real (CA 82), the Caltrain railway and the downtown commercial center. The academic and business hubs of Stanford and Palo Alto are to the east, while East Palo Alto and Redwood City are to the north and east. To the west, Menlo Park it is bordered by the estates of Woodside and Atherton.\nLeafy, suburban and with a mild year-round climate, Menlo Park is often considered to be one of the most pleasant towns on the San Francisco Peninsula. Northern portions of Menlo Park, north of US 101 tend to offer fewer attractions to visitors. In the south, Sand Hill Road is home to dozens of venture capital firms, which manage tens of billions of dollars for financing Silicon Valley technology companies that have become the economic backbone of Silicon Valley.\nThis article is primarily about Menlo Park's downtown area which is active during business hours. Since many businesses close by 8 or 9PM, visitors often turn to neighboring Palo Alto and Redwood City for late-night dining, beverage and entertainment options.\nGet in.\nDowntown Menlo Park is accessible by automobile, rail, bus and plane from San Francisco (30 miles or 50 km away) and San Jose (25 miles or 40 km away).\nBy automobile.\nPrimary access by car is via CA-82 (El Camino Real), US-101 (Bayshore Freeway) or the more scenic I-280 (Junipero Serra Freeway). Access from the East Bay, is via CA-84 and the Dumbarton Bridge. Menlo Park is served by local ride-hailing services like Uber and Lyft.\nBy rail.\nPerhaps, the easiest way to get to and from downtown Menlo Park is by using Caltrain.\nBy plane.\nMenlo Park is roughly equidistant (about a 30-minute drive) to the San Francisco International Airport (SFO) and the San Jose Mineta Airport (SJC). San Francisco's status as a major airline hub means that travelers are more likely to come through SFO.\nGet around.\nMenlo Park has an appealing and very walkable downtown area that is centered along Santa Cruz Avenue. It extends for about 10 blocks south (a 15- to 20-minute walk) from the Caltrain railroad station, across El Camino Real, and down to Camp Fremont Park.\nBiking is a good option for exploring beyond downtown Menlo Park. Biking can also be combined with the train or bus. Caltrain and the regional SamTrans or VTA bus systems can get you to further destinations. Ride sharing apps, like Uber and Lyft are also good, but more expensive options.\nMost Menlo Park residents drive a personal automobile when they are not walking or biking.\nBuy.\nThere are many shops in downtown Menlo, the ones listed here are unique or especially popular.\nDrink.\nCoffee and tea.\nMenlo Park has many coffee and tea shops. Local favorites are:\nAlcohol.\nMost restaurants in Menlo Park close by 9PM, so a late night drink can be a challenge. While nearly all restaurants in town serve wine and beer, and some have a bar, there are several places that stand out for liquor and cocktails.\nConnect.\nThe area code for Menlo Park and the surrounding area is 650.\nFree Wi-Fi is available at:\nGo next.\nMenlo Park is a good base of operations for visiting both Silicon Valley and San Francisco. It's close to Stanford, Palo Alto, and the Stanford Shopping Center."}
{"id": "21998", "revid": "235382", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=21998", "title": "Menominee", "text": "For the town in northwestern Wisconsin, see Menomonie.\nMenominee is a city in the Upper Peninsula of Michigan. It is across the Menominee River from Marinette, Wisconsin.\nUnderstand.\nMenominee was inhabited by the native American group of the same name, translating to \"Wild Rice\" in English, until 1836 where the Menominee and other native American tribes were moved west to other reservations, namely the Menominee Indian Reservation north of Shawano, Wisconsin. Menominee become more renowned in the mid-late 19th century as a lumber town much like with Marinette to the south, even going on to produce more lumber than any other city in the United States at the time, though the lumber industry waned around the 1910s. Since then, the industry has shifted towards manufacturing and tourism and the population has since been declining since the 1970 peak of 11,000 residents.\nGet in.\nBy car.\nPeople entering Menominee- and by that matter, Michigan- from the south will probably be on US 41, the main north-south route in town. US 41 connects Menominee with its southern twin city, Marinette, to the immediate south and further on to Green Bay, while taking a 65 mile course to the north for Escanaba. M-35 provides a shorter alignment to Escanaba at about 55 miles."}
